BATTLESHIP GAME + STATISTICS + HILL CIPHER

This project implements a complete Battleship-style game system with
post-game statistics and optional encrypted commands using the Hill
Cipher (modulo 37). The program is written entirely in C and respects
strict memory management and coding style constraints.

  -------------------
  GENERAL STRUCTURE
  -------------------

The application is split into three logical parts:

1)  GAME ENGINE (Task 1)
2)  STATISTICS PROCESSING (Task 2)
3)  ENCRYPTED COMMAND SYSTEM – HILL CIPHER (Task 3)

  ------------------------------------
  1) BATTLESHIP GAME ENGINE (Task 1)
  ------------------------------------

The game simulates multiple independent matches between two players. For
each match:

-   The board size is read (n x m)
-   The number of ships is computed automatically
-   Players place ships alternately
-   Invalid ship placements are rejected with a message
-   The boards are printed
-   Attacks are executed turn by turn
-   Each hit or miss is printed with a detailed message
-   Ships are tracked until destroyed
-   When all ships of a player are destroyed, the match ends

Each match collects: 
- Total number of hits 
- Total number of shots

  -------------------------------
  2) STATISTICS SYSTEM (Task 2)
  -------------------------------

After all matches are finished, aggregated statistics are stored:

For each player: 
- hits (lv) 
- total attacks (tt) 
- match weight

The program accepts commands of the form:

OUxxx.yyMIN / MAX OTxxx.yyMIN / MAX PUxxx.yyMIN / MAX PTxxx.yyMIN / MAX

Where: 
- O / P : Player 1 or Player 2 
- U / T : Single match or total game - xxx.yy : Target accuracy 
- MIN / MAX : Minimization or maximization strategy

The output format is always: AAA.BB.X

  ------------------------------------
  3) HILL CIPHER ENCRYPTION (Task 3)
  ------------------------------------

Commands and responses can be encrypted using Hill Cipher modulo 37.

Alphabet: - ‘0’–‘9’ → 0–9 - ‘A’–‘Z’ → 10–35 - ‘.’ → 36

Features: 
- Automatic matrix extraction from key 
- Modular determinant computation 
- Modular inverse (Extended Euclid) 
- Matrix adjugate -
Block-wise encryption/decryption

If the key is “-”: 
- Commands are read in plain text

Otherwise: - Commands are decrypted before processing - Results are
encrypted before output

  -------------------
  MEMORY MANAGEMENT
  -------------------

All dynamically allocated memory is explicitly freed: 
- Boards 
- Ships 
- Hill cipher matrices 
- Statistics buffers

No global variables are used.

  -------------
  COMPILATION
  -------------

Compile using:

gcc -std=c99 -Wall -Wextra main.c -o battleship

  --------------
  AUTHOR NOTES
  --------------

This implementation respects: 
- Strict 80-column rule 
- No global
state 
- Functional decomposition 
- Defensive memory management 
- Deterministic output format

